F1, GNLY, etc.). Pathways (Cell lysis, CD8+ T cell receptor, NK cytotoxicity, RAGE signaling). Validation identified 3 OFGs (KLRB1, NKG7, GZMH).\n    *   **Microbiome Connection:** None explicitly stated in the abstract. The card confirms \"未涉及微生物组因素\" (Does not involve microbiome factors).\n    *   **Relation to Gap:** The gap asks about *Prevotella*-specific mechanisms leading to myocarditis. This study discusses ICI-myocarditis mechanisms (CD8+ T cells, NK cells, specific genes) but *does not* mention *Prevotella*, FMT, or microbiome-derived antigens/metabolites. It describes the *downstream* immune pathology (cytotoxicity) but not the *upstream* microbial trigger.\n\n4.  **Evaluate Status & Usefulness:**\n    *   **Status:** `unmet`. The gap specifically asks for *Prevotella*-linked mechanisms or follow-up to FMT-LUMINate regarding microbial triggers. This study does not address the microbiome aspect at all.\n    *   **Material Usefulness:** `supporting`. While it doesn't answer the specific *Prevotella* question, it provides detailed information on the *downstream* molecular pathways of ICI-myocarditis (CD8+ T cell receptor, cytotoxicity genes) which might be the eventual pathway activated by any microbial trigger. It helps define the \"myocarditis\" endpoint mechanism even if the \"Prevotella\" trigger is missing.\n    *   **Useful Material:** The specific genes (KLRB1, NKG7, GZMH) and pathways (CD8+ T cell receptor, NK cytotoxicity) involved in ICI-myocarditis.\n    *   **Auxiliary Material:** The bioinformatics nature (prediction/hypothesis-generating), the drug warning (acetohydroxamic acid), the distinction between ICI-MC and other irAEs.\n    *   **Remaining Gap:** The specific link between *Prevotella spp.* (or any microbiome component) and these downstream pathways is not established in this text. The FMT-LUMINate follow-up regarding microbial antigens/metabolites is not addressed.\n    *   **Outline Action:** Use this to describe the effector mechanism of ICI-myocarditis (T cell cytotoxicity) while noting the upstream microbial trigger remains unidentified in this specific study.\n\n5.  **Drafting the JSON Content:**\n    *   *Status:* unmet (The specific *Prevotella* mechanism is not found).\n    *   *Material_usefulness:* supporting (Provides downstream context).\n    *   *Useful_material:* Focus on the identified immune pathways (CD8+ T cell receptor, NK cytotoxicity) and hub genes (KLRB1, NKG7, GZMH) as the potential downstream convergence point for any microbial trigger.\n    *   *Auxiliary_material:* Methodological limits (bioinformatics), specific dataset IDs (GSE180045), drug interaction warning.\n    *   *Remaining_gap:* No mention of *Prevotella*, FMT, or microbial metabolites/antigens. The upstream trigger is missing.\n    *   *Outline_action:* Cite as evidence for the final common pathway of ICI-myocarditis (cytotoxic T cell activation) while clarifying it does not resolve the microbiome-specific initiation question.\n\n6.  **Refining Constraints:**\n    *   Ensure I don't claim this study resolves the *Prevotella* gap.\n    *   Ensure I describe the study's actual findings first (bioinformatics on ICI-MC).\n    *   Ensure I don't infer microbiome connection where none exists.\n    *   Check JSON structure.\n\n7.  **Final Review against Constraints:**\n    *   \"Do not call the whole gap fulfilled while a stated success criterion remains open.\" -> Status is unmet.\n    *   \"Keep each finding attached to the study... actually reported.\" -> Will specify bioinformatics/DEG analysis.\n    *   \"Do not infer study design... from silence.\" -> Will state it's bioinformatics based on abstract.\n    *   \"Refer to the paper as 'this study' or 'the authors'.\" -> Will do.\n    *   \"Return one JSON object...\" -> Will do.\n\n8.  **Constructing the Text:**\n    *   *Study Description:* Bioinformatics analysis of ICI-MC vs controls. Identified DEGs, IR-DEGs, hub genes. Pathways: CD8+ T cell, NK cytotoxicity, 